const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are career development skills?',
    a: 'They are the skills that move your career forward, not only the skills you need to do today\'s tasks. In practice they fall into three groups: a core skill for your field, a multiplier skill that raises the value of that core skill (for example data, sales sense or AI-assisted workflow), and everyday professional habits such as clear writing and self-management. The right mix depends on what is currently holding your career back.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which career development skill should I learn first?',
    a: 'Start from the blocker, not the trend. If you are not getting shortlisted, build one visible, role-specific sample. If you get interviews but no offers, practise explaining real decisions and go deeper in one tool. If you are employed but not growing, take on a reporting, process or project task. If your pay is capped, look at a multiplier such as negotiation, analytics or a domain-plus-AI skill.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many skills should I work on at one time?',
    a: 'One main skill and, if you have the energy, one small supporting habit such as weekly written updates. Learning several skills at once usually leaves you with shallow notes on all of them and proof of none. Finish one visible piece of work, then decide what to add.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are soft skills or technical skills more important for career growth?',
    a: 'Neither works alone. Technical or domain skill gets you shortlisted and trusted with tasks. Communication, judgment and stakeholder handling decide how far the same skill takes you. Global employer surveys, including the World Economic Forum\'s, rank analytical thinking, resilience and leadership alongside AI and data skills for exactly this reason.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do certificates count as proof of a career development skill?',
    a: 'Sometimes, but rarely on their own. A certificate is stronger when the credential is specifically required or respected for the role. For most skills, a finished piece of work, a documented result or feedback from someone working in the field is stronger. Use the certificate as support for the work, not as a replacement for it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I work full time. How can I build a career development skill without burning out?',
    a: 'Plan around a small weekly block you can repeat, sized to your real week, rather than a heroic schedule that collapses after a few days. Aim the learning at something you can use on your current job, so the work itself becomes your practice and your proof. Some people need a few weeks to feel traction, others a couple of months. Both are normal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are there free government options for building skills in India?',
    a: 'Yes. Skill India Digital Hub lists many free courses. SWAYAM and NPTEL offer free course content, with a paid proctored exam if you want the certificate, and UGC has told institutions they may allow up to 40% of a semester\'s courses through SWAYAM. The AICTE National Internship Portal is free to register on and lists verified internships for students of AICTE, UGC and IGNOU institutions. Confirm current eligibility, fees and credit rules on each official site before you rely on them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between career skills and career development skills?',
    a: 'Career skills is the wider phrase for any skill used at work, including the ones that keep today\'s job running. Career development skills are the subset that changes what you can be hired or trusted for next: a visible core skill, a multiplier, and the human layer of judgment and communication.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the best career development skills for students?',
    a: 'Start with written English and spreadsheets in the first year, then one core skill for the field you are leaning towards, then a multiplier and a real internship task by the third year. The table for students above sets the order. The most useful proof is one finished project or internship task you can explain in two minutes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a career development skills matrix I can use?',
    a: 'Yes. The matrix above lists twelve skills with the source that supports each one, the blocker it helps most and the proof that counts. Pick the row that matches your blocker, build one visible piece of work, and test it with someone in the field.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which career development course is worth taking?',
    a: 'The one that ends in a finished project, gives feedback on your own work, is recently updated and fits your learning budget. Judge the specific course rather than the platform, try free material on the same topic first, and take the certificate only if your target employer asks for it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a career in skill development look like in India?',
    a: 'It usually means working as a trainer, assessor, learning designer in a company, or curriculum builder. Entry needs real skill in the subject first, and NSDC and the sector skill councils publish the trainer certification routes. Scheme-linked roles are often structured pay, while running your own niche cohort or corporate workshops has more upside.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it true that 85% of career success comes from soft skills?',
    a: 'No reliable source supports that exact split. The Carnegie Foundation says the figures were extrapolated from a 1918 study of engineering education. Current employer surveys do rank human skills such as analytical thinking, resilience and leadership as essential, while technical skills such as AI and cybersecurity are growing fastest, so build both.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a degree matter for career development skills?',
    a: 'A degree still matters where it is a legal or practical requirement, such as medicine, law or licensed engineering roles. For many other paths, employers increasingly look at what you can show you can do. The useful position is to keep the degree you have or need, and build a skill portfolio alongside it instead of relying on the degree alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from a skill assessment test?',
    a: 'A skill assessment gives you a readiness and role-fit signal about where you stand. Choosing what to build next is a separate step that starts from your blocker. Many people find it useful to take an assessment first, then use the Career Blocker Check to choose what to learn.',
  },
