            <td data-label="Proof that counts">{row.proof}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    One pattern shows up when the two World Economic Forum lists are set side by side. The top three
    core skills are human ones: analytical thinking, resilience and leadership. The top three
    fastest-growing skills are technical ones: AI and big data, networks and cybersecurity, and
    technology literacy. Human skills decide how far you go. Technical skills decide how fast demand is
    growing. A career development plan that has only one of the two is half built.
  </p>

  <h2 id="students">Career development skills for students</h2>

  <p>
    A student has one thing most professionals do not: several years before the first pay cheque. The risk
    is spending them on certificates. The table below sets the order that fits a four-year degree.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="student-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Year</th>
          <th>Weekly reality</th>
          <th>Build</th>
          <th>Expensive mistake</th>
          <th>Proof that counts</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {studentRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Year"><strong>{row.year}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Weekly reality">{row.weekly}</td>
            <td data-label="Build">{row.build}</td>
            <td data-label="Expensive mistake">{row.mistake}</td>
            <td data-label="Proof that counts">{row.proof}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Two facts make the plan easier than it looks. Government data shows that only 5.0% of people aged 15
    to 29 had formal vocational or technical training in 2025, so a finished project already sets you
    apart. And UGC told institutions in July 2026 that they may allow up to 40% of a semester's courses
    to be taken through SWAYAM. For a five-course semester that ceiling works out to two courses, if
    your college has enabled it.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A degree still opens doors and is required for many paths. It is not a complete career system.
      As a strict planning heuristic, not a research fact, aim to keep college to about a tenth of your
      total education and career budget, so money is left for tools, projects and later upskilling. Go
      well above that only for course-specific reasons such as a licence, labs or verified outcomes. That is how you build a high-income skill portfolio while
      still in college.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="foundation-floor">The foundation floor most people skip</h2>

  <p>
    Under any skill you choose sits a floor of ordinary habits. Nobody lists them on a CV, and
    interviewers notice their absence quickly.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clear written English.</strong> A short email that says what you need and by when. If English is your second language, write in short, plain sentences. Being clear matters more than sounding fancy.</li>
    <li><strong>Spreadsheets.</strong> Sorting, filters, simple formulas and a chart. It supports almost every field.</li>
    <li><strong>Safe AI use.</strong> Use AI to draft and research, then check the output yourself, and never paste confidential client or company data into a public tool.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-management.</strong> Keeping your own task list, meeting a deadline without reminders, telling your manager early when something slips.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you are early in your working life, the basics behind first-job hiring are covered in more detail
    in <a href="/blog/job-search/skills-needed-for-first-job-india/">skills needed for a first job in India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">What current data says about career development skills, and what it does not</h2>

  <p>
    Surveys can help you check direction. They cannot choose for you. Here is what five recent sources
    say, with the limit of each.
  </p>

