  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: World Economic Forum, Future of Jobs Report 2025; India Skills Report 2026 as reported by
    Careers360 and The Hans India; LinkedIn Skills on the Rise 2026 (global list on LinkedIn News, India
    stacks as reported by Storyboard18); TeamLease EdTech fresher hiring survey as reported by
    Careers360; Naukri JobSpeak, August 2026, as reported by ANI; Periodic Labour Force Survey 2025
    (Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation, via PIB).
  </p>

  <p>
    One more point from the LinkedIn reporting for engineers: as tools speed up building, the bottleneck
    moves from writing code to aligning people. That is why collaboration and process skills now sit
    beside technical ones in the rising lists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="myth">One statistic to stop repeating: "85% of success is soft skills"</h2>

  <p>
    You will see this claim credited to Carnegie or Stanford. The Carnegie Foundation says the figures
    were extrapolated from <em>A Study of Engineering Education</em>, written by Charles Riborg Mann and
    published in 1918. It is not a current finding, and it does not tell you how to split your time
    today.
  </p>

  <p>
    What current evidence supports is narrower and more useful. Employers in the WEF survey rate human
    skills as most essential, and the same survey shows technical skills growing fastest. Build both, in
    the order your blocker points to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-stack">Core skill, multiplier and the human layer</h2>

  <p>
    A durable skill profile has three layers. The core skill is what your field pays you for. The
    multiplier raises the value of the core skill. The human layer is the part that needs judgment, trust
    or explanation, and is harder for software to copy.
  </p>

